{{Japanese name|Konoe}}

{{nihongo|'''Shō Konoe'''|近衛 翔|Konoe Shō}} (12 October 1830 – 15 March 1896) was a Sanese statesman and court noble who served as the 2nd [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from July 1875 to March 1881. A member of the prestigious Konoe family, one of the five regent houses of the Imperial court, Konoe oversaw the implementation of the [[Saito Constitution]] and the establishment of the Imperial Diet. His primary foreign policy goal was to renegotiate the unequal treaties imposed by [[Dagit]], including the Treaty of Amity and Commerce, but he failed to secure any revisions and resigned under pressure. His premiership laid the institutional foundations of the modern Sanese state even as it exposed the limits of the nation's diplomatic leverage.

== Early life ==
Konoe was born in [[Okami]] in 1830 into one of the most illustrious families of the Imperial court. The Konoe house had served as regents (''sesshō'' and ''kampaku'') for centuries, and its members occupied the highest ranks of the court aristocracy. Young Shō was raised in the rarefied world of the Imperial palace, educated in the Chinese classics, calligraphy, and the intricate protocols of court ceremony.

The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 upended the world of the court aristocracy. The abolition of the feudal domains and the establishment of a centralized state destroyed the traditional power structures that had sustained the Konoe family's influence. Shō Konoe, then 41 years old, recognized that the old order was irretrievably lost and that the family's future depended on adapting to the new political reality.

Unlike many of his aristocratic peers who retreated into sullen opposition, Konoe embraced the Restoration with surprising enthusiasm. He saw in the new state an opportunity to transform the court from a ceremonial relic into a governing institution, and he offered his services to Emperor Saito as a bridge between the old aristocracy and the new political class.

== Political career ==
Konoe's entry into national politics was facilitated by his unique position as a court noble who supported the Restoration. Emperor Saito, who was keen to demonstrate that the new regime was not merely a military dictatorship, appointed Konoe to several key positions in the early government, including Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal and president of the committee charged with drafting the [[Saito Constitution]].

Konoe's contribution to the constitutional drafting process was significant. He advocated for a strong upper house—the House of Peers—that would represent the aristocracy and serve as a check on the popularly elected House of Commoners. He also argued for explicit constitutional protections for the Imperial institution, provisions that would ensure the Emperor's authority could not be undermined by parliamentary majorities.

When the first Prime Minister, [[Juichi Miyazaki]], resigned in July 1875, the Emperor appointed Konoe as his successor. The appointment was a deliberate signal that the new government was not exclusively dominated by the ''hanbatsu'' (clan faction) leaders from the Restoration provinces but also included representatives of the traditional court aristocracy.

== Premiership (1875–1881) ==
=== Implementing the Saito Constitution ===
[[File:Saito Constitution Implementation 1875.png|thumb|right|200px|The establishment of the Imperial Diet under the Saito Constitution]]
Konoe's primary domestic achievement was overseeing the implementation of the [[Saito Constitution]], which had been promulgated in 1875 but required extensive legislative and administrative work to put into practice. He established the Imperial Diet, convened the first elections for the House of Commoners, and organized the House of Peers from the ranks of the court aristocracy, the peerage, and the imperial appointees.

The implementation process was contentious. The Constitution's provisions for limited suffrage, the Emperor's reserve powers, and the independence of the military from civilian control were all subjects of intense debate. Konoe navigated these controversies with the diplomatic skill that was his hallmark, but the compromises required to secure passage of the implementing legislation left all parties dissatisfied.

=== Domestic policy ===
Beyond the constitutional framework, Konoe's government undertook a series of administrative reforms designed to consolidate the new state. The Local Government Act of 1876 established a uniform system of municipal and prefectural governance, replacing the patchwork of domain-era administrative structures that had persisted after the abolition of the ''han'' system. The Act created elected town and village assemblies—the first representative institutions at the local level—though their powers were strictly limited and subject to prefectural governor oversight.

Konoe also oversaw the establishment of the national education system. The Education Order of 1877, modeled on the Volisanian system, mandated compulsory schooling for all children and established a network of primary schools across the archipelago. The Order was a landmark in the nation's modernization, creating the literate workforce that would power the industrial expansion of the coming decades. However, the curriculum was heavily centralized, emphasizing loyalty to the Emperor and the state—a feature that would have lasting consequences for the character of Sanese civic culture.

The government's fiscal policy was constrained by the enormous costs of the Saito Restoration. The Land Tax Reform, which had been initiated under Miyazaki, was continued and refined under Konoe, providing a stable revenue base for the new state. However, the tax burden fell disproportionately on the rural population, contributing to the peasant unrest that would plague subsequent administrations.

=== The unequal treaties and foreign policy ===
[[File:Dagit Treaty Renegotiation 1878.png|thumb|right|200px|Konoe's diplomatic campaign to revise the unequal treaties with Dagit]]
Konoe's primary foreign policy goal was the renegotiation of the unequal treaties that [[Dagit]] and other Illypnian powers had imposed on the nation during the waning years of the Honda Shogunate. The Treaty of Amity and Commerce, which granted Dagit extraterritorial rights and fixed tariff rates, was widely regarded as a national humiliation, and its revision was the overriding diplomatic priority of the early Saito era.

Konoe launched a sustained diplomatic campaign to revise the treaties, dispatching envoys to Dagit, [[Moselle]], and [[Volisania]] with proposals for the gradual elimination of extraterritoriality and the restoration of tariff autonomy. He argued that the nation's adoption of Western legal codes and constitutional government had eliminated the justification for the unequal provisions.

The campaign was a failure. The Illypnian powers, which viewed the unequal treaties as essential to their commercial interests in the Shendan, refused to engage in substantive negotiations. Dagit's representative dismissed the Sanese proposals as "premature," while Mosellan diplomats offered only cosmetic concessions that would have left the fundamental inequalities intact. The failure was a devastating blow to Konoe's prestige and to the nation's confidence in its diplomatic standing.

In the Shendan, Konoe's government pursued commercial expansion as a means of building the economic strength necessary for future treaty revision. The Shendan Commercial Commission, established in 1878, provided subsidies and logistical support for Sanese trading companies seeking to establish operations in Shendan ports. The Commission's work laid the groundwork for the dramatic expansion of Sanese commercial influence in the region that would occur under subsequent administrations.

Konoe's government also faced early tensions with [[Shansi]] over the northern territories. The Shansian government, which had been expanding its own presence in the region, contested Sanese claims to several islands in the northern archipelago. Konoe attempted to negotiate a boundary agreement, but the Shansian position was inflexible, and the dispute remained unresolved—a source of friction that would intensify in subsequent decades.

=== Resignation ===
The failure of the treaty revision campaign, combined with growing criticism of the government's economic policies and its handling of the samurai rebellion of 1879, eroded Konoe's political support. The ''hanbatsu'' leaders, who had never fully trusted the court aristocrat, seized the opportunity to demand his resignation.

On 15 March 1881, Konoe tendered his resignation to the Emperor. He was succeeded by [[Toshiyuki Taguchi]], who served as interim prime minister before the appointment of [[Kanezane Enoki]] later that year.

== Legacy ==
Konoe's premiership is remembered for its institutional achievements and its diplomatic failures. The implementation of the Saito Constitution, the establishment of the Imperial Diet, and the organization of the peerage system were all foundational acts that shaped the nation's political development for decades. His advocacy for the Imperial institution within the constitutional framework established the principle that the Emperor was both the source of sovereign authority and a subject of constitutional constraint—a tension that would define Sanese politics until the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968.

The failure of the treaty revision campaign, however, cast a long shadow. It demonstrated that the nation's modernization had not yet earned it equal standing in the international order, and it fueled the nationalist resentment that would drive the nation's imperial ambitions in the following decades. The "Dagit humiliation," as the failed negotiations came to be known, was a rallying cry for every subsequent generation of nationalists.

After his resignation, Konoe retired from active politics and returned to the court, where he served as a senior advisor to the Emperor on matters of protocol and ceremony. He died in [[Okami]] in 1896 at the age of 65.

